well meanwhile hundreds of websites  including major news organizations and  the british government  have now started to work again after  going offline for about an hour  an outage at a cloud services company is  thought to have been to blame  the sites affected include the new york  times cnn the guardian and bloomberg  news  the san francisco-based company fastly  which hosts the websites  says it's now fixed the problem well  let's now speak to jody westby she is  the chief executive of global cyber risk  she joins us now  from washington dc jody how does  something like this happen  well this the good news is this was not  a cyber attack as far as we know  this was a configuration problem it was  a configuration service problem  and they they fixed that and were back  up  so it was a glitch in their system but  it shows how interconnected  we all are and how one problem  even in one small place can ripple  through  and spread across the globe and impact  you know millions of of internet users  well jody so many of the biggest  websites in the world are  by my understanding dependent on just a  few of these cloud servicing companies  that all sounds rather fragile can you  explain how that all works  think of it as a sandwich so you have  the  main service provider like google or new  york times or  guardian and then you have  the meat on the sandwich and on top you  have the customer  so fastly is like the meat in a sandwich  it is there to take the content and  deliver it very fast to the consumer  to the user up above and  vastly was impacted so the meat and the  sandwich like went away  and then the customer couldn't connect  and  so it was a service that is a very  important service in delivering content  around the world  and it was disrupted again because of  this configuration setting  and um and it impacted all these people  because it was such a critical layer  in the service delivery well as we were  saying earlier one of the sites that  went down was the british government's  site for instance are there security  concerns here i know you were saying it  wasn't an  attack but does this lay it open to to  perhaps  other attacks like this  um well i don't again as far as we know  this wasn't an  attack um so things can happen in  computer systems they're complicated and  there's  a lot of servers and routers and  switches and equipment  and networks all connecting and when  there  is a problem that has a ripple effect  then it  again it just shows how globally  connected we are  and how one small thing can have a large  impact  so sure i mean there's all kinds of  configuration setting issues or problems  this  obviously doesn't happen every day but  it is possible it could happen again and  it could be a different type of problem  jody doesn't outage like this have any  monetary or financial implications  oh yes so if you're a company that makes  money by the minute  so amazon for example ebay any of the  online services that are selling  uh goods or services um all of those  companies  lose money this is a business  interruption for them every  second that they're down and so many  companies  for example if you're processing credit  cards often those will get stored  in a in the server when you come back up  then they can process them  not thousands but usually they could  handle a few hundred  and so that isn't really a business  interruption but when you  can't make money and you lose those  minutes they're gone  so so it can have enormous business  interruption  losses even though this was a relatively  short period of an avenue  and a reminder as you say how connected  we are and how fragile all of this is  jody westby there the chief executive of  global cyber risk  great to get your expertise with us here  on al jazeera thanks for joining us jody
 

